# Proofs from the 2018 test suite (PropositionalTests.java) taken from
# students' first midterm exams. The format is described in ../README.md.
#
# The 2018 file also had parcial1ManuelSierraTuesday, commented out and
# unfinished: {(S → K) & (U → V)//W → (S | U)=>W → (T | V)}. It is not
# here because that argument is not valid (W, S, K true and T, U, V false
# make the premises true and the conclusion false), so it was probably
# copied with a typo.

case: midterm-1-manuel-sierra-tuesday-2
source: 2018 test parcial1ManuelSierraTuesday2, first midterm
problem: {S → (T → U)//U → ~U//(V → S) & (W → T)=>V → ~W}
4. ~U | ~U ; 2 IMPL
5. ~U ; 4 TAUT
6. (S & T) → U ; 1 EXP
7. ~(S & T) ; 6,5 MT
8. ~S | ~T ; 7 DM
9. (W → T) & (V → S) ; 3 COM
10. W → T ; 9 SIMP
11. S → ~T ; 8 IMPL
12. V → S ; 3 SIMP
13. V → ~T ; 12,11 HS
14. ~T → ~W ; 10 TRANS
15. V → ~W ; 13,14 HS
expect: valid

case: midterm-1-manuel-sierra-tuesday-3
source: 2018 test parcial1ManuelSierraTuesday3, first midterm, corrected
# Fixed: the last line was ~L & N by 4,13 CONJ, but the conclusion is ~L | N.
problem: {(L | M) | (N & O)//(~L & O) & ~(~L & M)=>~L | N}
3. ~L & O ; 2 SIMP
4. ~L ; 3 SIMP
5. ~(~L & M) & (~L & O) ; 2 COM
6. ~(~L & M) ; 5 SIMP
7. (~~L | ~M) ; 6 DM
8. L | ~M ; 7 DN
9. ~M ; 8,4 DS
10. ~L & ~M ; 4,9 CONJ
11. ~(L | M) ; 10 DM
12. N & O ; 1,11 DS
13. N ; 12 SIMP
14. ~L | N ; 4 ADD
expect: valid
legacy: DM collapses the double negation, so it gives L | ~M instead of ~~L | ~M at line 7

case: midterm-1-manuel-sierra-tuesday-3-as-written
source: 2018 test parcial1ManuelSierraTuesday3, first midterm, as written
problem: {(L | M) | (N & O)//(~L & O) & ~(~L & M)=>~L | N}
3. ~L & O ; 2 SIMP
4. ~L ; 3 SIMP
5. ~(~L & M) & (~L & O) ; 2 COM
6. ~(~L & M) ; 5 SIMP
7. (~~L | ~M) ; 6 DM
8. L | ~M ; 7 DN
9. ~M ; 8,4 DS
10. ~L & ~M ; 4,9 CONJ
11. ~(L | M) ; 10 DM
12. N & O ; 1,11 DS
13. N ; 12 SIMP
14. ~L & N ; 4,13 CONJ
expect: invalid at end: the last line is ~L & N, and the conclusion is ~L | N

case: midterm-1-rene-alejandro-tuesday
source: 2018 test parcial1RenéAlejandrotuesday, first midterm, corrected
# Fixed: redone step by step. The 2018 proof had DIST results with the
# disjuncts swapped (lines 7 and 12) and cited the wrong line from step 13 on.
problem: {~(S & T) → ~(A & (S | T))=>~(T → S) → ~A}
2. (A & (S | T)) → (S & T) ; 1 TRANS
3. ((A & S) | (A & T)) → (S & T) ; 2 DIST
4. ~((A & S) | (A & T)) | (S & T) ; 3 IMPL
5. (~(A & S) & ~(A & T)) | (S & T) ; 4 DM
6. (S & T) | (~(A & S) & ~(A & T)) ; 5 COM
7. ((S & T) | ~(A & S)) & ((S & T) | ~(A & T)) ; 6 DIST
8. ((S & T) | ~(A & T)) & ((S & T) | ~(A & S)) ; 7 COM
9. (S & T) | ~(A & T) ; 8 SIMP
10. ~(A & T) | (S & T) ; 9 COM
11. (~A | ~T) | (S & T) ; 10 DM
12. ((~A | ~T) | S) & ((~A | ~T) | T) ; 11 DIST
13. (~A | ~T) | S ; 12 SIMP
14. ~(A & T) | S ; 13 DM
15. (A & T) → S ; 14 IMPL
16. A → (T → S) ; 15 EXP
17. ~(T → S) → ~A ; 16 TRANS
expect: valid
legacy: DIST never turns p | (q & r) into (p | q) & (p | r)

case: midterm-1-rene-alejandro-tuesday-as-written
source: 2018 test parcial1RenéAlejandrotuesday, first midterm, as written
problem: {~(S & T) → ~(A & (S | T))=>~(T → S) → ~A}
2. (A & (S | T)) → (S & T) ; 1 TRANS
3. ((A & S) | (A & T)) → (S & T) ; 2 DIST
4. ~((A & S) | (A & T)) | (S & T) ; 3 IMPL
5. (~(A & S) & ~(A & T)) | (S & T) ; 4 DM
6. (S & T) | (~(A & S) & ~(A & T)) ; 5 COM
7. (~(A & S) | (S & T)) & (~(A & T) | (S & T)) ; 6 DIST
8. ~(A & S) | (S & T) ; 7 SIMP
9. (~(A & T) | (S & T)) & (~(A & S) | (S & T)) ; 7 COM
10. ~(A & T) | (S & T) ; 9 SIMP
11. (~A | ~T) | (S & T) ; 10 DM
12. (S | (~A | ~T)) & (T | (~A | ~T)) ; 11 DIST
13. S | (~A | ~T) ; 11 SIMP
14. (~A | ~T) | S ; 12 COM
15. ~(A & T) | S ; 13 DM
16. (A & T) → S ; 14 IMPL
17. A → (T → S) ; 15 EXP
18. ~(T → S) → ~A ; 16 TRANS
strict: invalid at 7: DIST gives ((S & T) | ~(A & S)) & ((S & T) | ~(A & T)), with each disjunct the other way round
flexible: invalid at 13: SIMP needs a conjunction, and line 11 is a disjunction
